==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|grc|ὑπό|t=under}} + {{m|grc|φύσις|t=nature}}, from {{m|grc|φύω|t=to bring forth}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/haɪˈpɒfɪsɪs/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|hypophyses}}

# {{lb|en|anatomy}} The [[pituitary gland]].
#* '''1915''' - Wilhelm Falta, (trans. Milton Kayton Meyers) ''[http://books.google.com/books?id=sGEPAAAAYAAJ The Ductless Glandular Diseases]'', P. Blakiston's Son & Co. [http://worldcat.org/wcpa/oclc/4634141 OCLC 4634141], page 231
#*: CHAPTER VI / THE DISEASES OF THE '''HYPOPHYSIS''' <nowiki>[PITUITARY GLAND]</nowiki> <!-- Square brackets in original-->
# {{lb|en|botany}} The top cell of the [[suspensor]] in a [[dicot]] [[embryo]], which will [[differentiate]] to form part of the root cap.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|author=Trevor A. Thorpe|title=In Vitro Embryogenesis in Plants|publisher=Springer|isbn=0792331494|page=85
|passage=All the cells of the suspensor, except one, are eventually crushed and obliterated by the growing embryo; this fortunate cell known as the '''hypophysis''' is the one in immediate contact with the embryo and plays an important role in the formation of the root apex.}}

